===Noun===
{{en-noun|head=[[B]]-[[girl]]}}

# {{lb|en|US|slang}} A woman employed to talk to customers in a [[bar]] and encourage them to buy drinks; a [[hostess]].
#*'''a. 1969''', {{w|John Kennedy Toole}}, ''A Confederacy of Dunces'':
#*:Clearing his throat, Patrolman Mancuso stood before his sergeant and said, “I got a lead on a place where they got '''B-girls'''.”
#* {{quote-text|lang=en|year=1996|author=Russell Means|title=Where White Men Fear to Tread|url=http://print.google.com/print?hl=en&id=Fk7jzBdB8MMC&pg=PA133&lpg=PA133&sig=b4Kt78euQMQeHWpsA5wCnOeVsJc|passage=The bar was a rough, tough frontier place with '''B-girls''' drinking colored water at premium whiskey prices, bought by horny guys who thought they were going to get laid but were only going to get screwed.}}
#* '''1997,''' Dan Wakefield and Sara Davidson, ''Going All the Way'' [http://print.google.com/print?hl=en&id=TLpV2M22PAIC&pg=PA286&lpg=PA286&sig=q-PAz6nqIiPYZ0BbK6ib_uLkduU]:
#*: What’s really sick, if you ask me, is the '''B-girl''' shit where you pay all that money for just looking and thinking about it, but not really doing it.

===Etymology 2===
{{m|en|break|b(reak)}} + {{m|en|girl}}, formed by analogy with {{m|en|B-boy}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|head=[[B]]-[[girl]]}}

# A woman who performs [[breakdance]]; a female [[breaker]].
# A female member of the [[hip-hop]] [[subculture]].

